          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Higher geometry
            or
            %em homotopical geometry
            is the study of concepts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          .un_remark{:style => "border:solid #0000cc;background: #add8e6;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %h6#slogan Slogan
            %p
              higher geometry =
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
              +
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %p
            Higher geometry subsumes notably the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+stacks"} geometric stacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stacks"} derived stacks
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
            and it subsumes at least parts of (derived)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
            below.
          %p
            In any given instance of higher geometry, one starts with a notion of “local models” for the geometry. An
            %em affine space
            will then be a formal dual of such a local model, and a general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            will be formed by “gluing” these affine spaces in some appropriate way. There are two ways of formalizing this idea, coming from
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Alexander Grothendieck
