              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} derived noncommutative algebraic geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
          %h3#ConnesStyleNoncommutativeGeometry Connes-style noncommutative geometry
          %p
            The process of forming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+algebras"} groupoid convolution algebras
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
            from suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+stack"} topological
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stacks"} differentiable stacks
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+bimodules"} Hilbert bimodules
            between them. Much of
            = succeed "-style" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes"} Connes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            turns out to deal with objects in the image of this functor, and to the extent that it does, Connes-style noncommutative geometry may be regarded as being a way of speaking about higher geometry, specifically the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stacks"} differentiable stacks
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structures"} higher structures
            %li
              %p
